WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:15.980
Tim Ferriss: Hello ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.000
This is Tim Ferriss and this is yet another
edition of The Random Show, episode number

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
4,732.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Kevin Rose.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.760
Kevin Rose: We've done quite a few.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.269
Tim Ferriss: Serial entrepreneur.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:27.269
Amazing.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:28.269
Good fella.

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:29.269
Generous soul.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:30.269
Kevin Rose: Thank you.

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:31.269
Tim Ferriss: In spirit.

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.160
Kevin Rose: [inaudible 00:01:27] doing these
podcasts together.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.410
We did quite a few episodes.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.559
Tim Ferriss: Avid wearer of trucker hats.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:40.559
Kevin Rose: Yes.

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:44.340
Tim Ferriss: And drinker of red wine as we've
already established by the sound effects that

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.340
preceded this introduction.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.340
Kevin, nice to see you.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.129
Kevin Rose: Yeah, it's good to see you as
well.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:50.629
We need to do this in person more often.

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:51.629
We did one in the last year.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:52.629
Tim Ferriss: We do.

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:53.629
It was so fun.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:54.870
It was so fun to do it in person.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.450
Kevin Rose: I know.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.870
Tim Ferriss: So what nectar of the gods are
you suckling over there?

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:00.870
Kevin Rose: Yes.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.110
I'm having a little bit of The Mascot from
Napa Valley.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:11.420
The Harlan family, we know Will, obviously,
over there and they are quality producers

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.080
of quite fine cabernet red wine that is just
amazing.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.390
This is my favorite.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.369
To be fair, I'm conflicted a little bit because
I am on their advisory board, but they make

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:25.369
great wine.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:27.430
Tim Ferriss: They do make great wine.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.430
Kevin Rose: It's fantastic wine.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.159
Tim Ferriss: I've been over yonder back when
I was living in NorCal.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:35.340
You and I had quite a bit of that even before
you were on the board.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.640
Very, very tasty grapes.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.700
Tasty, tasty grapes.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.700
Kevin Rose: Absolutely.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.120
Tim Ferriss: They can put that on the label,
if they want, as a quote.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.320
Kevin Rose: Tasty Grapes from Tim.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.329
[inaudible 00:02:38] back, founder of CØCKPUNCH.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:46.329
Tasty Grapes.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:47.329
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:51.079
Kevin Rose: I'm sure that'll do wonders for
their floor price of their—

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:59.829
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, for that very high
and top shelf legacy, multi-generational branding

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:00.829
that they're going after.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:01.829
I think that would really help.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:02.829
Kevin Rose: Yes.

00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:03.829
That's what they want is tasty grapes.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:04.829
Tim Ferriss: Tasty grapes.

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:05.829
I am drinking...

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:07.542
I don't know if you've ever seen this before,
this thing-

00:02:07.542 --> 00:02:08.542
Kevin Rose: No.

00:02:08.542 --> 00:02:09.640
Tim Ferriss: ... which I'm drinking.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:16.770
It's this little can of R 1,3 Ginger Mule
hard ketones.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.110
So this is ethanol-free-
Kevin Rose: Oh, Jesus.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.110
Tim Ferriss: ... alcohol.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.110
Hold on.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.110
I have other ethanol alcohol.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.300
Kevin Rose: You're drinking hard ketones now?

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.180
That's where we're at?

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.060
Tim Ferriss: Hold on.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.060
Pause.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.030
Don't knock it until you've tried it.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.930
Kevin Rose: I'm not going to [inaudible 00:03:27].

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.850
Tim Ferriss: So R 1,3 Butanediol, I know where
you're going to take that.

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.450
So the R 1,3 Butanediol, a few things.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.640
Number one is you do get drunk on this stuff.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.530
You cannot drive if you drink this stuff-
Kevin Rose: Right.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.349
Tim Ferriss: ... all alcohol, pretty much.

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:53.080
Like all alcohol, but the point being that
it is not ethanol so you are avoiding some

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:59.610
of the metabolites and issues through liver
metabolism that you run into with regular

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.610
alcohol.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.990
It is also an appetite suppressant so if you're
used to drinking and then just housing five

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:11.550
pizzas like I did the other night, then this
is a counterbalance.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:12.730
And what I've been constantly [inaudible 00:04:12].

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:13.940
Kevin Rose: It speeds you up though.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:14.940
It's ketones, right?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.360
So it's like coke alcohol.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.849
Tim Ferriss: Well, here's the thing.

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:24.530
No, it's ketones but it's not beta hydroxybutyrate
or a performance-enhancing ketone.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.150
This is a hard ketone intoxicant.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:28.650
So you have to be very careful.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:29.739
They have warnings.

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:32.250
No pregnant ladies, et cetera, all this stuff.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.920
Do not consume more than three day.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.890
Kevin Rose: Oh, my god, dude.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:37.890
[inaudible 00:04:35].

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.500
Tim Ferriss: You legitimately get intoxicated
when you drink this, so you have to be very

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.740
careful but it contains-
Kevin Rose: Does it burn when you pee?

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.360
Tim Ferriss: No, it does not burn with when
you pee.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:54.530
It does not give you horns or vestigial tail
or eyelid hair, none of those things, and

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.200
I have been enjoying experimenting with this
because what I will sometimes do, and I haven't

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.329
done this much, but I will have one of these-
Kevin Rose: You're an investor, I can tell.

00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:03.329
Look, you're sipping [inaudible 00:05:02]
investment.

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:04.519
Tim Ferriss: I am not an investor.

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.120
Yeah, this is like the Wayne's world [inaudible
00:05:09] drinking Pepsi-

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.450
Kevin Rose: You're like, "I will have one
of these every night at 7:00 PM."

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.819
Tim Ferriss: No, I am not an investor.

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:16.060
I have no vested interest.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:23.360
Unlike that shill, Kevin Rose, I have no vested-
Kevin Rose: I said I was involved.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.850
I almost spit the grape juice that you called
it or whatever you-

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:26.850
Tim Ferriss: Tasty.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:27.850
Kevin Rose: Tasty grapes, yeah.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.560
Tim Ferriss: So yeah, I am not involved.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:36.820
I have no problem hawking the stuff when I
am, but this is actually very tasty.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:42.930
So Ginger Mule tastes like a Moscow Mule,
but it has no ethanol-based alcohol in it,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.110
and here's what I'm going to do.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.850
So I'm going to have this first.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:50.800
Hopefully, and this is, I think, perhaps wishful
thinking, but I'm hoping the ketones will

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:58.060
have some neuroprotective effect so that when
I then shift into fifth gear to have this,

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.800
which is a tequila I've become very, very
fond of...

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.220
I'm not involved, but I have been drinking
it because I had it first at a restaurant

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:17.360
called Suerte here in Austin which has excellent
TexMex food, very high end, outstanding cuisine.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:24.051
And the first time I had this that the server
recommended was at that restaurant when I

00:05:24.051 --> 00:05:26.530
was having dinner with Chase Jarvis and we
were both blown away.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:27.530
Kevin Rose: I love Chase.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.030
Tim Ferriss: Chase is a great guy.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.030
Chase [inaudible 00:06:29].

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.030
Kevin Rose: [inaudible 00:06:29].

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.610
Tim Ferriss: Both wonderful humans, and we
were both really impressed with this and I

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.900
enjoyed it so much that I ended up buying
a bottle, which is quite rare for me because

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.080
I don't drink that much but I do really like
tequila.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:46.110
I mean, if you were in Texas and you drink
alcohol, you owe it to yourself to explore

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.580
the range of tequila, and this stuff is outstanding,
it's just fantastic.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.580
This will be step two.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.080
Kevin Rose: I have two questions for you.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:54.840
Actually, one question.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.190
You can cut this later if you want.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.350
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.759
Kevin Rose: You said it on a private text
to me that you had a couple of drinks with

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:01.889
Attia last night.

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:06.250
Attia is not a big drinker, at all.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:09.060
Every time I've gone out to dinner with him,
he has one tequila.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.500
Tim Ferriss: I had more than he had.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.500
I had more than Peter.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.910
Kevin Rose: Is he into this ketone thing as
well?

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:15.910
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.180
Kevin Rose: Was that what you were drinking
or no?

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.320
Tim Ferriss: No, no, no.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.479
We were drinking red wine from Spain, which
was fantastic in the moment.

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:25.780
I love red wine.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.860
I'll continue to have it on occasion.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.960
Maybe I'm just becoming the crypt keeper,
if anybody gets the reference.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.190
You've got to be pretty old already to get
the reference.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:43.759
My body tolerates wine of any type increasingly
poorly with respect to sleep.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:49.780
If I have three glasses, man, I feel like
somebody put a pillowcase over my head and

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.910
just punched me in the face for like an hour
in the middle of the night.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:53.910
It's rough.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.349
Kevin Rose: Do you get the hot sweats?

00:06:56.349 --> 00:06:58.699
Tim Ferriss: I do, reliably.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:02.500
And you can track it if you have an Oura ring
or something else on, you look at it, you're

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.330
like, "Good lord, what happened to my body
in the middle of the night?"

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.330
It is so bad.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:07.330
Kevin Rose: Yeah, this happens to Daria, too.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.770
She's like a sweaty line backer in the middle
of the night.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.320
It's like you don't want to go near it.t
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's brutal.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:12.320
Kevin Rose: Yeah, it's a lot.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.500
Tim Ferriss: And I know that's going to happen,
which is why if I'm going to have a drink,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.440
a real drink or otherwise...

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:21.710
I have not found this stuff to help sleep,
by the way.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:28.680
The R 1,3, as much as I am interested and
I have been, I have boxes of this stuff, and

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:36.009
I'm not an investor, because I'm interested
in how I might use this to moderate ethanol-based

00:07:36.009 --> 00:07:39.740
alcohol consumption, which I enjoy.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.669
But let's just say I can pregame with one
of these, get to a point where I'm kind of

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:45.669
loopy.

00:07:45.669 --> 00:07:53.870
I will say that one of these compared to one
say tequila soda, the R 1,3 will fuck up my

00:07:53.870 --> 00:08:00.949
verbal acuity or my ability to speak much
more than the-

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:02.150
Kevin Rose: Yeah, that's all you're really
looking for.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.759
Tim Ferriss: ... real alcohol.

00:08:03.759 --> 00:08:04.759
So it's perfect for this podcast.

00:08:04.759 --> 00:08:07.509
Kevin Rose: At the end of the day you just
want to be slurring your words.

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:14.680
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and this stuff also per
can, I have found to throw off your physical

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.580
coordination a lot more than an equivalent
single alcohol drink, so you do need to be

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.169
careful with it.

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:25.850
It's not something you want to pound and then
grab a chainsaw and go out gathering wood.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:30.639
Kevin Rose: Well, that's the weird thing is
it's such a non-obvious...

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:33.800
Like I would never have imagined you were
going to say that when you started this podcast,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:39.680
because typically I think ketones is like,
"Okay, I'm going to chug a bottle pre-workout,"

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.089
so I mean I guess you could go lifting right
now, drunk lifting if you wanted to, but-

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.380
Tim Ferriss: No, this would be terrible for
performance.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.630
This is bad for performance.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.890
Well, let's put it a different way.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:55.269
It is not a performance enhancer in the same
way that other ketone supplements might be,

00:08:55.269 --> 00:08:57.120
so I have taken...

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:02.380
Even earlier today, I took a different exogenous
ketone supplement because I was eating pretty

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:09.800
low calorie today before recording a podcast
this morning, and it, within 20 minutes or

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:17.550
25 minutes, reliably kicked up my blood ketone
levels as would be measured with, say, a

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:21.440
Kevin Rose: [inaudible 00:10:18] strip or-
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no, the Precision Xtra.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:22.440
Kevin Rose: [inaudible 00:10:20].

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.000
Tim Ferriss: You can use a breathalyzer, you
can use the finger pricks.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:33.300
I didn't measure it, but reliably I will see
a bump of one to two millimoles, which is

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.300
substantial.

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:42.140
And I went from being foggy and tired and
slow to being fast with my words, not having

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.420
to search for turns of phrase, everything
was coming quickly.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.020
It was very reliable as a performance enhancer.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.730
This, what I'm drinking, will have the opposite
effect.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:54.710
Kevin Rose: So actually this is really interesting.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:10:00.340
One question for you on that front, Tim, because
I know having traveled with you a ton to countries

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:06.860
where you've been so generous as to be our
translator, where Tim will translate for you

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.520
for about six hours and then you'll just shut
down

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.200
Tim Ferriss: And then I get so grumpy.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.930
Kevin Rose: You're, "You figure it out.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.100
I'm done translating."

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.490
Tim Ferriss: "Google Translate's good enough,
God damn it.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.279
I don't want to do this job anymore."

00:10:21.279 --> 00:10:22.279
Kevin Rose: Exactly.

00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:26.160
Tim was like our paid translator at one of
my birthday parties for the entire trip.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.279
Unpaid, unpaid.

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:34.140
So I'm curious though, you know mentioned
how quick...

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.029
Words just flow like butter.

00:10:36.029 --> 00:10:39.090
Would it help in a situation like that, do
you think?

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:40.410
Have you tried it?

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:41.680
Ketones when translating?

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.519
Tim Ferriss: For translating, I'm sure it
would help if you're using the right class

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:46.769
of ketone.

00:10:46.769 --> 00:10:50.720
Because when we're talking about ketones,
you have ketone esters, you have ketone salts,

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.970
and I'm not qualified to talk about the intricate
science of it all.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:11:00.970
Someone like Dr. Dominic Agostino, who I've
had on the podcast at least twice, is one

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:06.120
of the preeminent researchers in the space
who also works with...

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:12.089
I want to say the Department of Defense, might
be DARPA, in synthesizing compounds for special

00:11:12.089 --> 00:11:13.210
forces and [inaudible 00:12:12].

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.130
Kevin Rose: Yeah, I was going to say weren't
the Seals using it at some point?

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:23.490
Tim Ferriss: Right, and so they need measurable
benefit, and one of the measurable benefits

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:30.540
is that it helps you to sustain physical and
cognitive performance under conditions of

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:38.200
low oxygen, which you might experience if
you're doing, say, extended deep diving or

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:41.610
submersion, which you might also experience
if you're at altitude.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:50.779
So recently, this would've been early October,
I used ketones to help acclimate to altitude

00:11:50.779 --> 00:11:57.300
and then tapered off of the ketones so I wouldn't
perhaps prevent some of the physiological

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:01.920
adaptations that I would want to sustain without
using ketones every day.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.120
So they're fascinating.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:10.410
I will say, if I'm taking the other side of
the argument and making a counter case, I

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:18.810
am skeptical that taking exogenous ketones
constantly when you are consuming a normal

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.380
diet, which is relatively rich in carbohydrates...

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:28.120
So your blood glucose is not necessarily pathologically
elevated, but if your glucose is normally

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:35.621
elevated and then you have very elevated blood
ketone levels, my understanding is that does

00:12:35.621 --> 00:12:44.639
not readily occur in nature, at least with
humans in an organic capacity, unless you

00:12:44.639 --> 00:12:52.730
are in a dangerous position as a diabetic
where you're experiencing keto acidosis, which

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:53.730
can be fatal.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.209
I mean it's very, very precarious.

00:12:56.209 --> 00:13:01.519
So my feeling is, if this doesn't occur in
nature, because normally you'd be producing

00:13:01.519 --> 00:13:04.990
ketones in a state of starvation even though...

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:06.209
I should be clear.

00:13:06.209 --> 00:13:11.519
We're producing ketones a lot of the time
but in trivial amounts.

00:13:11.519 --> 00:13:15.089
