that is really disappointing to me is in the
last year, actually a couple years now since

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:58.700
COVID, Apple Watch will have a cardio fitness
level built inside of it and mine's been slowly

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.510
going down and I'm like, "Ah, shit.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:02.510
This sucks.

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:06.080
I got to get back into shape."

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:09.000
My dad died of a heart attack, my grandpa
died of a heart attack.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:14.450
Cardiovascular issues are not new to my family
so, for me, this is a big deal.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:23.420
So I'm really focused on cardiovascular fitness
for this new year.

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:25.159
A couple of things just to let people know
where I'm at.

00:26:25.159 --> 00:26:29.940
I've tried out all the different latest versions
of software for fitness tracking.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.530
I'd like to link this up in your show notes.

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:38.909
There's a scientist on YouTube called the
Quantified Scientist that I really like.

00:26:38.909 --> 00:26:40.120
He's awesome.

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:46.880
He basically uses a lot of the gold standard
ways to track sleep and he'll hook himself

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:51.809
up to all the real professional devices and
then also use the consumer devices and say,

00:26:51.809 --> 00:26:53.430
"How good are these devices?

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:57.240
How accurate are they at really predicting
the levels of sleep?

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.370
How good are they at heart rate detection
while jogging?

00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:03.520
Is Oura any good because it's moving around
on your finger all the time."

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:06.620
There's all these open questions, and all
he does...

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:10.179
I think it's his full-time job now... is just
put out new videos with all the latest fitness

00:27:10.179 --> 00:27:13.730
trackers and tell you if they're any good
and a lot of them are crap.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:15.360
Tim Ferriss: That's a cool niche, though,
that he's basically saying-

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:16.669
Kevin Rose: Yeah, it's a really cool niche.

00:27:16.669 --> 00:27:20.860
Tim Ferriss: He's like, "All right, we're
going to look at hydrostatic underwater weighing

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:24.860
for body composition and compare it to whatever
this thing is you're wearing."

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:26.929
If we take it as...

00:27:26.929 --> 00:27:31.620
I think I'm getting that description right,
but if we take this underwater approach as

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:35.800
the, say, Olympic gold standard, how do these
things stack up?

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:36.800
That's cool.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:37.800
Kevin Rose: Right, exactly, exactly.

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:38.800
Yeah, it's really cool.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:43.809
And so the TLDR is that the Apple Watch is
actually pretty damn good now.

00:27:43.809 --> 00:27:49.299
It didn't always used to be, but it's really
good for heart rate and it's really good for

00:27:49.299 --> 00:27:50.309
sleep.

00:27:50.309 --> 00:27:52.960
The WHOOP I like because...

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:58.330
It's not as good as the Apple Watch, but what
it does is it does activity-specific tracking,

00:27:58.330 --> 00:27:59.580
so for example...

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:01.620
And yes, you can do that on the Apple Watch
too.

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:05.409
You can say, "I'm doing elliptical," or, "I'm
doing all this stuff," but the WHOOP is cool

00:28:05.409 --> 00:28:07.600
because you can say, "Okay, I'm going to sit
down.

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.620
I'm going to do a meditation."

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:13.130
It'll detect that you're doing that and then
it'll give you your heart rate in real time,

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:18.250
or it'll show you the graph after you're done
over that activity, and it's much harder to

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:20.419
try and find that data.

00:28:20.419 --> 00:28:25.260
Apple, their device is amazing, their software
kind of sucks.

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:29.470
Like health kit and the whole health interface,
it's not a consumer app.

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:31.659
It's very clinical feeling.

00:28:31.659 --> 00:28:35.620
So the data's there, but it's not represented
in the right way, if that makes sense.

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:41.000
So anyway, long story short, Oura has a brand
new sleep algorithm.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:45.540
The Quantified Scientists have yet to test
that out against all those other things.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:48.370
Their sleep algorithm, I think, was pretty
screwed up before, but they're testing a new

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:49.419
one now.

00:28:49.419 --> 00:28:53.330
You can enable it in beta so I'm going to
wait and see how that goes for sleep tracking.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:57.740
But anyway, my methods for cardio, Peloton
Rower.

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:02.250
I'm not involved in Peloton in any way, don't
have any stock but I saw the new rower and

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:03.380
it looks pretty interesting.

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:06.049
So they give you a 30-day money back guarantee.

00:29:06.049 --> 00:29:07.260
I just bought it.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.779
It'll show up in the next couple of days.

00:29:09.779 --> 00:29:11.030
I think that's good.

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:16.530
They say rowing is 80% of your muscles, which
is amazing, all at the same time, so if you're

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:20.799
looking for a quick hit, like 15, 20-minute
workout...

00:29:20.799 --> 00:29:22.390
I don't know, I'm excited about it.

00:29:22.390 --> 00:29:23.750
I'm going to give it a shot.

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:25.140
Tim Ferriss: I can hop in for a second.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:26.140
Kevin Rose: Yeah, please.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:31.809
Tim Ferriss: So I had a chance to test the
Peloton Rower for the first time, and this

00:29:31.809 --> 00:29:39.160
is part of due diligence because I have been
having conversations with Peloton, and you

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:41.299
didn't know this before putting together-
Kevin Rose: No.

00:29:41.299 --> 00:29:42.299
Tim Ferriss: ... the prep for this conversation.

00:29:42.299 --> 00:29:44.564
Kevin Rose: This is the first I'm hearing
of it.

00:29:44.564 --> 00:29:48.750
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, so Peloton and I have been
talking about possibly doing something in

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:53.630
terms of sponsorship on the podcast related
to the rower, and as is my first step with

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:59.039
all of these things, I asked to use a unit,
I said I have to test it.

00:29:59.039 --> 00:30:01.890
And I had the chance to test a unit.

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:07.010
This would've been, I don't know, two or three
weeks ago.

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:08.910
And I was very impressed with it.

00:30:08.910 --> 00:30:14.080
And I can give you a couple of tips that might
help other people who are also getting the

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:15.080
units.

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:22.010
So the first is the form correction is very
helpful, and almost everyone will use their

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:27.780
arms too much, lean back too far, and use
their legs incorrectly.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:31.710
Kevin Rose: So I don't know what the form
correction is, it's just like in the software

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:33.220
or something like-
Tim Ferriss: It is in the software.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:41.289
So it will show a profile of you on the rower
and it will flash red in areas where you need

00:30:41.289 --> 00:30:43.890
to correct the form-
Kevin Rose: How does it know to break?

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:44.899
The front camera?

00:30:44.899 --> 00:30:50.299
Tim Ferriss: Sensors in the seat and sensors
on the pulling mechanism.

00:30:50.299 --> 00:30:53.490
And there are probably other sensors on top
of that.

00:30:53.490 --> 00:30:56.049
And the feedback is actually quite helpful.

00:30:56.049 --> 00:31:01.980
There is a competitive or former competitive,
I think he's still current competitive rower,

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:04.770
very large white guy who has excellent form.

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:10.330
And there are other instructors who have excellent
form, but he is the instructor who I know,

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:14.179
and I'm blanking on his name, people could
figure it out, who has a competitive background.

00:31:14.179 --> 00:31:19.269
And the reason that's attractive to me is
if you are going to be a competitive rower

00:31:19.269 --> 00:31:22.480
in the long term and succeed-
Kevin Rose: No injuries.

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:23.480
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:29.909
If your technique is not efficient, you are
going to suffer from repetitive use injuries,

00:31:29.909 --> 00:31:32.409
100%, and debilitate you.

00:31:32.409 --> 00:31:37.519
So I like the idea of modeling, at least in
the beginning stages, the technique on someone

00:31:37.519 --> 00:31:39.070
who has competitive experience.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:40.610
So that would be a recommendation.

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:42.470
I apologize, I'm blanking on his name.

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:47.519
The last thing I would say, and I've provided
feedback, product feedback, so we'll see,

00:31:47.519 --> 00:31:52.610
since I think they could make modifications
quite easily with firmware instead of changing

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:53.610
the hardware.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:32:00.870
And if my experience with the Peloton bike
is any indication, they will make constant

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:02.809
firmware improvements.

00:32:02.809 --> 00:32:09.059
So I expect by the time I'm even saying this,
that once you get your shipped unit, I'm hoping

00:32:09.059 --> 00:32:10.850
there may be some modifications made.

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:17.059
The sensors at the very back of the seat are
very sensitive.

00:32:17.059 --> 00:32:27.300
So my experience was, you may get a bit too
much feedback in terms of a red alert indicating

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:32.179
you're leaning back when you may not in fact
be leaning back that far.

00:32:32.179 --> 00:32:38.519
So two suggestions, and these are my experiential
suggestions, not any type of, I don't think

00:32:38.519 --> 00:32:40.090
official suggestion.

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:44.179
Scoot your butt forward just a little bit
so that it's not all the way at the back of

00:32:44.179 --> 00:32:45.480
the seat.

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:51.750
Second is absolutely focus on not leaning
back excessively.

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:57.080
And so I do think as a practice, especially
for engaging your legs, it's good to almost

00:32:57.080 --> 00:33:01.520
lean forward slightly as you do the exercise.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:10.559
And what blew my mind is once I had the technique
feedback, once I had the guidance in terms

00:33:10.559 --> 00:33:16.240
of exertion and some metrics to watch, I found
that I could do longer rowing workouts.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:19.590
I could actually do a long rowing workouts.

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:23.899
And I have a concept two rower, and I like
the concept two rower.

00:33:23.899 --> 00:33:29.600
But what I found is without any of those feedback
mechanisms, I would crank up the resistance

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:35.220
because there's a resistance style on the
concept two, crank up the resistance, and

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:42.620
I would go balls to the wall for 500 meters
and I would effectively be done.

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.010
My heart rate would be through the roof.

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:50.559
My arms would be sore, my legs would not be
sore, and I was roasted.

00:33:50.559 --> 00:33:52.250
I could go longer.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:58.510
But if I were trying to do something, let's
just say as a short morning workout, 500 to

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:00.480
750 meters.

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:06.990
And I was like, okay, I think that at this
point my biceps are the weak link in the chain

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:15.230
using the Peloton rower and following the
feedback easily could do a 30 to 45-minute

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:21.560
workout, which better engaged all of the musculature
that you were aiming to engage.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:24.951
So I had a very positive experience, very
positive experience, and I'm sure that they

00:34:24.951 --> 00:34:26.889
will of course.

00:34:26.889 --> 00:34:30.329
And they already, I'm sure, have many plans
to iterate on the firmware.

00:34:30.329 --> 00:34:31.329
So I think you'll enjoy it.

00:34:31.329 --> 00:34:33.020
I'll be curious to hear your thoughts.

