00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:44.770
I still think I'm a 15-year-old skateboarder
and I start doing stuff at the park and getting

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:47.280
fancy and then I injure myself, inevitably.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:54.260
So rather than try to rely on restraint, which
is not my strong suit, I just don't allow

00:36:54.260 --> 00:36:56.560
myself to snowboard anymore.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:08.569
Skiing is a wonderful combination of time
and nature, being outside, moving with speed

00:37:08.569 --> 00:37:15.839
under control, where you get an adrenaline
hit, but ideally without excessive danger.

00:37:15.839 --> 00:37:24.619
It is subtle, and you can refine technique
for a long time and not run out of room to

00:37:24.619 --> 00:37:26.500
improve.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:30.530
You can make quantum leaps in your performance
with proper instruction.

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:33.200
Kevin Rose: And you can do it when you're
70 too.

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:35.200
People can-
Tim Ferriss: And you can do it when you are

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:36.200
older.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.890
And that has become so much more important
for me.

00:37:38.890 --> 00:37:45.810
And to this end though I am, or both in process
and leading up to it.

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:48.060
So let's just say I have actually not that
much time.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.400
I have 10 days, so I'm not going to do a whole
lot of conditioning in the next 10 days.

00:37:51.400 --> 00:37:59.420
But I have done some leading up to it focusing
on lower body, lower leg.

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:03.540
As you know, I've had so many injuries, so
I've torn most of the connective tissue, the

00:38:03.540 --> 00:38:09.829
meaningful connective tissue in my ankles
over the years just from the outrageous decisions

00:38:09.829 --> 00:38:16.099
and bad commitments with combat sports and
getting heel hooked and things like this.

00:38:16.099 --> 00:38:26.210
So I, as a result, need to compensate by strengthening
the lower legs on the lateral portion, the

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:31.990
rear of my calf, everything to try to compensate
for weak ankles and terrible torso flexion.

00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:34.260
And-
Kevin Rose: Is that your weak point if someone

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:36.610
wanted to take you out is just go for your
ankles?

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:40.471
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you got to go for the ankles.

00:38:40.471 --> 00:38:41.471
Kevin Rose: Just curious.

00:38:41.471 --> 00:38:42.471
Tim Ferriss: It is a weak spot.

00:38:42.471 --> 00:38:43.471
So yeah, you can go for the ankles.

00:38:43.471 --> 00:38:47.620
If someone wants to come at me, go for the
ankles and I'll just pull out my concealed

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:50.210
carriage and shoot you in the face anyway.

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:53.920
Kevin Rose: [Inaudible 00:40:07] punch floor
price, just go for his ankles.

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:57.140
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, if you go for the ankles,
just be prepared.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:38:59.250
I may be packing.

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:00.690
So there's that.

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:06.650
And the reason I bring all of this up though
is that it's one thing for me to make commitment

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:13.369
in say an urban environment to improve lower
body strength and to work on stability.

00:39:13.369 --> 00:39:20.560
And to do so in a way that is sort of clinical
in a workout without real world engagement.

00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:28.450
But if I really want to stack the deck in
my favor to accomplish that, I want to set

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:29.869
the environment.

00:39:29.869 --> 00:39:36.780
And I want to set my social interactions in
such a way that that goal is served.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:43.380
And I recognize this is a very fortunate position
that I'm in, but I do think people can borrow

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:49.210
this maybe framework or way of looking at
goals to think about.

00:39:49.210 --> 00:39:56.109
All right, I say I want to do X, how can I
optimize spending time with people who will

00:39:56.109 --> 00:40:01.050
help me achieve X, or who are already achieving
X, or are trying to achieve X?

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:08.140
How can I put myself in locations where I
am more likely to interact with those people

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:17.440
and absorb some of these maybe ancillary habits
related to my primary goal through sheer exposure.

00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:22.119
Kevin Rose: So Tim tells that, I think that
is such a very, very important thing.

00:40:22.119 --> 00:40:28.220
This idea that the sum of your closest friends
largely makes up what you're into, your hobbies,

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:31.280
the things you get excited about.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:37.250
When you find there's something new you want
to go after, let's call it skiing or something.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:41.290
You're lucky because you can pick up the phone
and world class skiers will answer and say,

00:40:41.290 --> 00:40:42.780
let me take you out for the day.

00:40:42.780 --> 00:40:45.290
So you're Tim Ferriss.

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:50.590
But how do you recommend that people going
into the new year, if there's something new

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:54.470
they want to get into, or they look around
at their friend group and they're just like,

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:56.660
this isn't helping me get to the next level.

00:40:56.660 --> 00:40:58.530
What do you do if you're stuck like that?

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:00.980
Tim Ferriss: Go make some new friends.

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:05.020
And I would say furthermore let's-
Kevin Rose: Friendster.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:06.670
Tim Ferriss: Friendster.

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:13.640
So aside from Friendster, I will say, and
I'll mention this, I don't mention it that

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:14.640
much.

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:19.150
The 4-Hour Chef confusingly is actually a
book about accelerated learning and skill

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:20.200
acquisition.

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:22.580
I talk a lot about this in The 4-Hour Chef.

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:27.069
So even if you buy it, you don't give a shit
about cooking, and you just want to learn

00:41:27.069 --> 00:41:31.819
about meta learning and acquiring physical
skills, there's a section called meta learning

00:41:31.819 --> 00:41:33.540
in The 4-Hour Chef.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:38.720
And I highly recommend that people dig into
it because the recommendations work.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:41.569
And I've used them dozens of times now.

00:41:41.569 --> 00:41:48.400
One of the points that I make is if you go
on Google and search whatever your target

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:52.340
sport is, let's just for the time being assume-
Kevin Rose: I want to play that pickle sport.

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:53.340
Tim Ferriss: Pickleball?

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:55.930
Kevin Rose: Yeah, I haven't tried that yet.

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, pickleball is fun.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:41:59.490
Pickleball is fun.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:04.870
It's more ankle friendly than tennis, but
you can still roll an ankle or two if you're

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:05.870
not careful.

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:07.220
So it is exciting.

00:42:07.220 --> 00:42:16.819
Austin is also not only the third coast capital
of ayahuasca and unending ayahuasca conversations,

00:42:16.819 --> 00:42:18.480
but also pickleball.

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:20.850
There's a lot of pickleball here.

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:24.770
And in sort of Central Valley, California,
it's become very popular.

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:29.130
But putting pickleball aside for a second,
because that is not yet an Olympic sport.

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:36.821
Let's say you want to get better at a sport
that is a professional sport, or I should

00:42:36.821 --> 00:42:41.290
say amateur or professional sport represented
in the Olympics.

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:48.460
Could be swimming, could be skiing, could
be just about anything, snowboarding, et cetera.

00:42:48.460 --> 00:42:59.270
If you search for snowboard silver medalist,
bronze medalist, or even maybe gold medalist

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:06.300
from two Olympics ago, there's a very good
chance, and this is not to minimize their

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:10.950
achievement, but the fact of the matter is
there are many sports that are financially

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:16.270
rewarded in the US, basketball, baseball,
and there are many that are not.

00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:18.770
And I don't think that's fair in sense.

00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:23.579
It's not reflective of the dedication or skill
necessary to be good at certain things.

00:43:23.579 --> 00:43:26.150
Gymnastics would be another one.

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:30.280
However, that does mean an opportunity for
a lot of people.

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:37.020
And you could find a silver medalist or a
bronze medalist who by the way, in many cases

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:39.960
is just a gold medalist who had a bad day.

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:41.880
That's it.

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:50.619
And you could get one-on-one remote or in-person
training with them in many cases for a hundred

00:43:50.619 --> 00:43:51.650
bucks an hour.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:56.619
And I realized-
Kevin Rose: That's amazing.

00:43:56.619 --> 00:43:57.940
I haven't thought about that.

00:43:57.940 --> 00:43:58.940
Yeah.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:00.560
Any medal is fine, like bronze, copper, whatever.

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:04.030
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you don't need, also, if
you are...

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:09.500
I mean, I am not the best skier, but I do
ski black diamond, I've done some hell skiing

00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:13.420
and I've done a lot of off piece, kind of
off terrain.

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:21.750
I'm not a great skier, but I have a basic
foundation of ability.

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:23.819
I do not need a gold medalist.

00:44:23.819 --> 00:44:27.700
I need someone who is substantially better
than I am.

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:29.760
And this is very key.

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:32.859
And who can teach.

00:44:32.859 --> 00:44:40.500
Because in many cases you'll find, say a college
coach, maybe they're retired, doesn't really

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:46.270
matter, in a given sport who will be a far
better instructor and get you further ahead

00:44:46.270 --> 00:44:47.609
than a gold medalist.

00:44:47.609 --> 00:44:50.240
If that gold medalist has never really taught.

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:53.809
Everything they've done since age five has
become second nature.

00:44:53.809 --> 00:44:58.060
So they can't explicitly name or describe
what they're doing.

00:44:58.060 --> 00:45:05.809
So you have a broad menu of people you can
choose from if you're trying to learn a new

00:45:05.809 --> 00:45:06.809
skill.

00:45:06.809 --> 00:45:15.069
And it is so mind bendingly affordable in
so many cases.

00:45:15.069 --> 00:45:17.420
There's so many fields.

00:45:17.420 --> 00:45:18.839
Let's take an example.

00:45:18.839 --> 00:45:21.470
I am very excited about archery.

00:45:21.470 --> 00:45:25.829
So some of my goals relate to archery for
the new year, and I've been taking it seriously

00:45:25.829 --> 00:45:27.100
for a while.

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:28.810
I feel very comfortable with compound.

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:31.520
I'm focusing now on recurve and bare bow which
is-

00:45:31.520 --> 00:45:35.490
Kevin Rose: Are you better than Attia or is
Attia better than you because he's in there

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:36.490
as well.

00:45:36.490 --> 00:45:37.490
Tim Ferriss: I don't know.

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:38.490
I don't know.

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:39.490
I've no idea.

00:45:39.490 --> 00:45:44.960
I mean, Attia has gone full with archery.

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:50.470
He's making his own materials-
Kevin Rose: He's better than you.

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:51.470
Tim Ferriss: .... fletching.

00:45:51.470 --> 00:45:52.630
Well then, he might be.

00:45:52.630 --> 00:46:00.410
Then, he might be, I am feel very confident
in my ability to do what I have tasked myself

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:03.270
with doing with respect-
Kevin Rose: We got into bareback riding a

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.960
long time ago, that archery, remember that?

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:07.940
You were doing bareback archery in Japan.

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:12.770
Tim Ferriss: It is not bareback, but I was
doing yabusame horseback archery in Japan

00:46:12.770 --> 00:46:15.760
with traditional bows that are long bows,
about six feet long.

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:17.330
Where you-
Kevin Rose: Is there a saddle on that horse

00:46:17.330 --> 00:46:18.330
or no?

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:20.830
Tim Ferriss: There's a saddle, but the saddle
is very, very thin wood.

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:25.940
The only purpose of the saddle is to hor hold
the stirrups on the horse so you can ride

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:29.130
the horse without sitting on the horse.

00:46:29.130 --> 00:46:32.819
So you're effectively doing a wide squat in
the stirrups.

00:46:32.819 --> 00:46:38.109
The only purpose of the saddle is to hold
you on the horse.

00:46:38.109 --> 00:46:40.849
And the saddle is not intended to be sat upon.

00:46:40.849 --> 00:46:44.109
I have two antique saddles at home-
Kevin Rose: I know.

00:46:44.109 --> 00:46:45.109
I've seen those.

00:46:45.109 --> 00:46:46.109
Tim Ferriss: ... that were my reward.

00:46:46.109 --> 00:46:54.010
Those were my first real reward I gave myself
in any meaningful way for anything I did.

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:58.540
And it was for The 4-Hour Body hitting number
one New York Times.

00:46:58.540 --> 00:46:59.680
That's why I have the saddles.

00:46:59.680 --> 00:47:00.680
Yeah.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:03.520
Kevin Rose: And so, gosh, Tim, we've known
each other a long time, man.

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:04.809
Tim Ferriss: Super long time.

00:47:04.809 --> 00:47:11.430
Kevin Rose: I remember walking in, what was
that downstairs place that you had, you went

00:47:11.430 --> 00:47:17.660
down this hallway to Tim's house and it was
like there was a big huge Japanese, you had

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:19.380
the full armor, right?

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:20.619
Off to the light.

00:47:20.619 --> 00:47:21.619
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:47:21.619 --> 00:47:22.619
I had the armor.

00:47:22.619 --> 00:47:23.619
Kevin Rose: What was that?

00:47:23.619 --> 00:47:24.619
Little kendo you had or something?

00:47:24.619 --> 00:47:27.390
Tim Ferriss: So the armor had traveled with
me for quite a while.

00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:29.329
This was kendo armor that I used in Japan.

00:47:29.329 --> 00:47:32.160
Kevin Rose: A kendo you had in San Francisco
with that little...

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:34.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it was on the right hand
side.

00:47:34.040 --> 00:47:39.490
So as you walked in, it was in two different
places.

00:47:39.490 --> 00:47:43.069
One place I rented, and then another place
that I bought.

00:47:43.069 --> 00:47:46.240
And the place that I bought, which was in
Glen Park, I guess both of them were in Glen

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:48.320
Park.

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:53.880
The first one had this hallway leading in,
pretty long hallway, and it was all kettlebells.

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:55.050
It was all kettlebells.

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:57.309
It was just like 20 kettlebells.

00:47:57.309 --> 00:48:01.210
And then you walk up and the armor was to
the right.

00:48:01.210 --> 00:48:07.530
It was directly to the right, across this
walkway that went from the living room into

00:48:07.530 --> 00:48:08.950
the kitchen.

00:48:08.950 --> 00:48:15.730
And the armor was directly on the right when
you came into the place that I bought later.

00:48:15.730 --> 00:48:21.329
You'd walk in and on the left hand side was
an entire chalkboard wall that had artwork

00:48:21.329 --> 00:48:22.329
on it.

00:48:22.329 --> 00:48:25.520
So it was like 12 feet of chalk art, which
was incredible.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:26.520
So good.

00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:28.400
I was so impressed with this artist.

00:48:28.400 --> 00:48:31.230
And then on the right hand side had the Japanese
armor.

00:48:31.230 --> 00:48:33.720
And that was the kendo armor I used when I
was in Japan.

00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:36.260
Kevin Rose: Tim's house back then was exactly...

00:48:36.260 --> 00:48:40.579
Because I met you and you just launched the
4-Hour Workweek.

00:48:40.579 --> 00:48:42.119
It was just the launch party.

00:48:42.119 --> 00:48:43.309
It was when I met you for that.

00:48:43.309 --> 00:48:47.829
And I remember I came to your house, and I
remembered just walking at some point to hang

00:48:47.829 --> 00:48:49.940
out, and I didn't know you that well.

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:53.119
And it was just like you walked on this aisle
of kettlebells.

00:48:53.119 --> 00:49:01.400
It was like a hall of kettlebells, and then
this Japanese armor and weird saddles and

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:02.680
maybe the saddles were later I think.

00:49:02.680 --> 00:49:04.319
Tim Ferriss: Saddles were few years later,
yeah.

00:49:04.319 --> 00:49:05.319
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

00:49:05.319 --> 00:49:08.490
And then there was books all over the place
with all these little note things in them.

00:49:08.490 --> 00:49:11.460
And I'm just like, this guy is crazy.

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:17.570
Tim Ferriss: The honeymoon phase of our friendship.

00:49:17.570 --> 00:49:18.750
Kevin Rose: That's right.

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:20.720
Tim Ferriss: Kettlebell date.

00:49:20.720 --> 00:49:30.589
So yeah, I suppose taking a long roundabout
way of saying, do not assume that you do not

00:49:30.589 --> 00:49:38.320
have access to really good teachers, it's
right there.

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:42.960
But people don't look for it because they
assume that it's not.

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:51.119
And this type of outreach and finding instructors
I did well before I ever had the first book.

00:49:51.119 --> 00:49:52.349
And you can do it.

00:49:52.349 --> 00:49:58.930
There are very direct ways to do it as particularly
if you know the right questions to ask, you

00:49:58.930 --> 00:50:00.780
need to know the right questions to ask.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:08.510
It is your job to use the instructor like
the niche expert Google you want them to be,

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:09.680
to provide feedback.

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:13.380
You have to know how to be proactive as a
student.

00:50:13.380 --> 00:50:19.040
And if you learn how to do that, you also
remove one of the requirements I gave.

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:24.470
And that is you can take someone who is not
experienced in teaching and make them a good

00:50:24.470 --> 00:50:28.109
teacher by virtue of feeding in the right
questions.

00:50:28.109 --> 00:50:33.680
And I don't want to sound like I'm hitting
it too hard because frankly I make next to

00:50:33.680 --> 00:50:35.680
nothing on these books.

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:40.510
But the 4-Hour Chef meta learning section
has all of this.

00:50:40.510 --> 00:50:41.510
It's very in depth.

00:50:41.510 --> 00:50:42.589
So I encourage people to check it out.

00:50:42.589 --> 00:50:43.589
Kevin Rose: Awesome.

00:50:43.589 --> 00:50:44.589
Very cool.

00:50:44.589 --> 00:50:45.589
Tim Ferriss: What else you got?

00:50:45.589 --> 00:50:49.369
Kevin Rose: You can do a couple more resolutions
or?

00:50:49.369 --> 00:50:50.589
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, let's do it.

00:50:50.589 --> 00:50:56.750
I've shifted into fifth gear, and I'm making
the transition to the [inaudible 00:52:18].

00:50:56.750 --> 00:50:57.750
Just FYI.

00:50:57.750 --> 00:51:01.309
Kevin Rose: I'm on my second and a half class,
so it's going to get interesting real quick

00:51:01.309 --> 00:51:02.309
here.

00:51:02.309 --> 00:51:04.880
All right, so a few more things on my list
and then we can move.

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:10.740
I want to talk to you about your NFT ventures
and figure out how that's going.

00:51:10.740 --> 00:51:15.790
So I finished my seven-day very first ever
Zen meditation retreat.

00:51:15.790 --> 00:51:18.540
It was fantastic in so many ways.

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:23.060
And I would say that the one thing that it
gave me is when you sit for eight hours of

00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:31.770
meditation per day, you realize very quickly
that the 25 minutes is actually nothing, it

00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:34.220
just is nothing.

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:41.520
And it has really, really helped me create
since returning from that trip, I had just

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:44.310
haven't missed a day, and it's just been solid.

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:49.260
Because it feels like it's so trivial, it's
like of course I can find 25 minutes to go

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:56.480
and sit, or do a double sit in a day and get
to 50 or 55 if you're doing walking along

00:51:56.480 --> 00:51:57.480
with it.

00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:01.200
But it just reframed that entire thing for
me, which I think is fantastic.

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:07.970
And it also gave me this sense of just knowing
I want to go deeper in this realm.

00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:15.569
And the experience of the community and at
the Zendo and everyone getting together.

00:52:15.569 --> 00:52:17.589
You've been on one of these.

00:52:17.589 --> 00:52:24.780
And I know yours was a little bit rocky, I'd
be curious to see if you'd go back to one,

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:26.820
but I had a fantastic time in.

00:52:26.820 --> 00:52:32.450
So I know meditation is going to be top priority
and of all the things that I do going into

00:52:32.450 --> 00:52:33.450
next year.

00:52:33.450 --> 00:52:35.830
Tim Ferriss: So let me ask some follow ups.

00:52:35.830 --> 00:52:40.010
I will say that I would be open to doing something
like that again.

00:52:40.010 --> 00:52:41.500
I would probably try a different format.

00:52:41.500 --> 00:52:50.780
So at the time I'd done a Vipassana silent
retreat, and the sitting was similarly intensive

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:57.450
and the vast majority of your day is sitting
or walking meditation, mostly sitting.

00:52:57.450 --> 00:53:01.059
And I've told that story before so I won't
tell it here.

00:53:01.059 --> 00:53:08.740
If people want to hear that, and effectively
it gets into some very heavy stuff so I won't

00:53:08.740 --> 00:53:09.740
rehash it here.

00:53:09.740 --> 00:53:13.960
But if you want to hear the story, I told
it on 10% Happier, the podcast with Dan Harris.

00:53:13.960 --> 00:53:15.760
And we got into it quite a bit.

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:24.240
I had effectively a full psychotic unraveling
at my extended meditation retreat, which relates

00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:28.560
to a bunch of childhood trauma and abuse and
so on.

00:53:28.560 --> 00:53:36.230
So it's not what I would consider family listening,
but if people want background, they can listen

00:53:36.230 --> 00:53:37.230
to that.

00:53:37.230 --> 00:53:40.819
And it ended up being a very important experience
for me, but it also scared the shit out of

00:53:40.819 --> 00:53:41.819
me.

00:53:41.819 --> 00:53:45.990
And I was very worried about being in a psychological
free fall.

00:53:45.990 --> 00:53:52.641
And thank God Jack Kornfield, who has been
on this podcast a number of times, who is

00:53:52.641 --> 00:54:03.400
an extremely masterful and skilled teacher,
meditator, who is clinical psychologist, has

00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:12.790
worked with veterans, adolescents who cut,
he has an extremely hybridized toolkit, which

00:54:12.790 --> 00:54:15.750
makes him very, very, very effective.

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:17.750
And he walks the walk.

00:54:17.750 --> 00:54:20.170
That guy is as legit as it comes.

00:54:20.170 --> 00:54:23.829
So people may also want to listen to the podcast
I've done with him.

00:54:23.829 --> 00:54:26.160
I would be open to testing another format
though.

00:54:26.160 --> 00:54:34.119
And I've been extremely impressed with Henry
Shukman, who is your Jedi master as I understand

00:54:34.119 --> 00:54:35.119
it.

00:54:35.119 --> 00:54:36.510
And-
Kevin Rose: He's amazing.

00:54:36.510 --> 00:54:40.390
He's been on your podcast twice now, highly
recommended to go back and listen.

00:54:40.390 --> 00:54:43.220
Tim Ferriss: He has, and I wanted to thank
you for that introduction.

00:54:43.220 --> 00:54:44.630
You had recommended it.

00:54:44.630 --> 00:54:45.630
And he's outstanding.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:49.079
He is a very, very good teacher.

00:54:49.079 --> 00:54:51.570
He's a compelling speaker.

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:58.880
And I could see delving into a different format
to see what that provides.

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:01.359
And I'm not in a rush to do it.

00:55:01.359 --> 00:55:06.869
I am, however, feeling some urgency to begin
meditating again.

00:55:06.869 --> 00:55:08.790
I've completely fallen off the horse.

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:12.099
And so that has been at the very top of my
list.

00:55:12.099 --> 00:55:15.480
And I continue to find excuses to put it off.

00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:21.680
And it's funny because this happens to me
once every year or two where I'll be meditating,

00:55:21.680 --> 00:55:29.210
meditating, meditating, and then suddenly
it's like the excuse factory took a vacation

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:33.589
for a year and then it's like, "I'm back."

00:55:33.589 --> 00:55:36.829
And all of a sudden I fall off the horse and
I stop.

00:55:36.829 --> 00:55:41.470
