side, and hopefully have something ready for

01:07:52.270 --> 01:07:56.230
our conference in May, just for fun.

01:07:56.230 --> 01:08:04.140
And then the last thing I would say, and it's
something that's really important to me is,

01:08:04.140 --> 01:08:08.990
I want to do this yearly delete of things.

01:08:08.990 --> 01:08:12.940
And I have this rule and tell me, Tim, if
you have a better idea-

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:13.940
Tim Ferriss: Physical things.

01:08:13.940 --> 01:08:14.940
Kevin Rose: Physical things.

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:15.940
Yeah.

01:08:15.940 --> 01:08:21.159
And so what I'm thinking is that if I haven't
used something in six months, or maybe I should

01:08:21.159 --> 01:08:25.699
even shorten it to three months, let's call
it clothing, devices, et cetera.

01:08:25.699 --> 01:08:28.920
Something that's sitting around in your house.

01:08:28.920 --> 01:08:30.740
Just donate it.

01:08:30.740 --> 01:08:36.199
Donate it, give it away to Goodwill, let it
find the home it belongs in and reduce your

01:08:36.199 --> 01:08:41.850
stuff to like, I'd love to cut in half, to
be honest.

01:08:41.850 --> 01:08:43.830
I don't need all the crap.

01:08:43.830 --> 01:08:45.759
How do you feel about that?

01:08:45.759 --> 01:08:46.759
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:08:46.759 --> 01:08:52.090
I have some policies for myself that have
been very helpful I think.

01:08:52.090 --> 01:08:58.069
And sometimes I over-correct and I'll explain
why over-correcting makes sense, at least

01:08:58.069 --> 01:08:59.660
for me.

01:08:59.660 --> 01:09:06.179
The first is, and I'm sure you get this, but
I think I get it to a much greater degree.

01:09:06.179 --> 01:09:09.509
Maybe when you were at the helm of Digg, this
was an issue.

01:09:09.509 --> 01:09:11.429
I get sent so much shit.

01:09:11.429 --> 01:09:12.509
Kevin Rose: Yes.

01:09:12.509 --> 01:09:14.310
Tim Ferriss: It is unbelievable.

01:09:14.310 --> 01:09:17.210
I just get mountains of stuff sent.

01:09:17.210 --> 01:09:19.719
Kevin Rose: We're in the same boat, dude.

01:09:19.719 --> 01:09:21.941
Yeah, that's part of why I was thinking about
it.

01:09:21.941 --> 01:09:24.550
It's like you get a lot of books, you get
a lot of swag-

01:09:24.550 --> 01:09:25.550
Tim Ferriss: Stuff.

01:09:25.550 --> 01:09:26.550
Kevin Rose: You get a lot of things.

01:09:26.550 --> 01:09:27.550
Yeah, exactly.

01:09:27.550 --> 01:09:28.550
Tim Ferriss: You get a lot of stuff.

01:09:28.550 --> 01:09:35.140
And so one of my rules, and I don't follow
it perfectly, but some rules followed imperfectly

01:09:35.140 --> 01:09:38.450
still add a lot of benefit.

01:09:38.450 --> 01:09:43.839
And in my case, if I'm going to keep something
new, I try to get rid of something.

01:09:43.839 --> 01:09:48.170
So if I'm going to accept one thing in, one
thing's going to go.

01:09:48.170 --> 01:09:55.449
If somebody sends me, maybe it's a really
comfortable shirt that has some clever thing

01:09:55.449 --> 01:09:59.740
on it and there's some really minimal branding
for their company and it's a friend's thing

01:09:59.740 --> 01:10:02.449
and I'm like, "Okay, this is a comfortable
shirt.

01:10:02.449 --> 01:10:04.130
Maybe I'll keep this."

01:10:04.130 --> 01:10:09.679
I will try to find a shirt that I get rid
of because I have simply too much stuff.

01:10:09.679 --> 01:10:14.239
I will also go through periods of purging.

01:10:14.239 --> 01:10:17.560
And I try to do it leading into the winter.

01:10:17.560 --> 01:10:24.100
Because, frankly, if you're out there and
you have warm clothing, meaning clothing for

01:10:24.100 --> 01:10:25.330
cold weather.

01:10:25.330 --> 01:10:31.010
If you have layers or thicker clothing or
longsleeve shirts that you are not using and

01:10:31.010 --> 01:10:36.600
you will not use this winter, holy shit, there
are people who need that.

01:10:36.600 --> 01:10:44.340
There are people who are homeless, there are
people who are without means to buy clothing

01:10:44.340 --> 01:10:46.520
for themselves or their children.

01:10:46.520 --> 01:10:47.679
Donate that stuff.

01:10:47.679 --> 01:10:49.140
And here's what I would say.

01:10:49.140 --> 01:10:52.699
If you're on the fence, give it away.

01:10:52.699 --> 01:10:57.310
And I don't want to do too much of the Marie
Kondo stuff, but it's like, if it doesn't

01:10:57.310 --> 01:10:58.490
spark joy, here's what I would say.

01:10:58.490 --> 01:11:00.240
This is another-
Kevin Rose: I love your accents by the way.

01:11:00.240 --> 01:11:01.770
They're so good every time you do one.

01:11:01.770 --> 01:11:02.770
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.

01:11:02.770 --> 01:11:06.370
Yeah, if anybody didn't know, it's not Marie
Kondo, even though it's very convenient for

01:11:06.370 --> 01:11:07.739
the English-speaking market.

01:11:07.739 --> 01:11:08.830
It's Maria, Maria, Maria Kondo.

01:11:08.830 --> 01:11:09.929
Kevin Rose: Maria Kondo.

01:11:09.929 --> 01:11:14.110
Tim Ferriss: And yeah, she has the most perfect
skin of all time, by the way.

01:11:14.110 --> 01:11:15.820
People can find a photograph of me with her.

01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:16.820
I interviewed her in Japan.

01:11:16.820 --> 01:11:19.780
Kevin Rose: Was it figured out what her secret
is?

01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:23.170
Tim Ferriss: Genetics maybe.

01:11:23.170 --> 01:11:31.290
It's like an AI created the perfect human
porcelain doll, it's unbelievable.

01:11:31.290 --> 01:11:32.290
I don't know what it is.

01:11:32.290 --> 01:11:33.290
Kevin Rose: Are you attracted to her?

01:11:33.290 --> 01:11:34.290
Tim Ferriss: She's cute.

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:35.290
Oh, absolutely.

01:11:35.290 --> 01:11:38.690
She's also married and has a family and that's
not something I'm going for.

01:11:38.690 --> 01:11:42.330
But she's a good looking woman.

01:11:42.330 --> 01:11:43.780
Jesus Christ.

01:11:43.780 --> 01:11:45.230
All right.

01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:48.190
Anyway, no, but let me come back to this process.

01:11:48.190 --> 01:11:51.150
Kevin Rose: And I have a question for you,
but go ahead.

01:11:51.150 --> 01:11:52.150
About buying stuff.

01:11:52.150 --> 01:11:53.550
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for sure.

01:11:53.550 --> 01:12:02.889
So what I try to do when I look in, whether
it's my closet or at things I own, is ask

01:12:02.889 --> 01:12:10.440
on a scale of say one to 10, how much joy
am I getting out of this?

01:12:10.440 --> 01:12:14.160
Or how much use am I getting out of this?

01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:18.350
And then the follow-up question is, no matter
what the answer is, if it's a 10, I keep it.

01:12:18.350 --> 01:12:19.790
If it's a nine, I'd probably keep it.

01:12:19.790 --> 01:12:21.870
If it's an eight, I'd probably still keep
it.

01:12:21.870 --> 01:12:25.860
But if it's less than that, could I give this
to someone?

01:12:25.860 --> 01:12:32.000
Could I donate this to someone who would absolutely
get an eight, nine, or 10 use or joy out of

01:12:32.000 --> 01:12:33.000
this?

01:12:33.000 --> 01:12:34.280
If so, get rid of it.

01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:44.510
And if you have some means, and that doesn't
mean millions of dollars, but getting rid

01:12:44.510 --> 01:12:49.360
of a T-shirt or two is probably not going
to break the bank for a lot of people.

01:12:49.360 --> 01:12:51.780
In which case, when in doubt, donate it.

01:12:51.780 --> 01:12:53.840
Give it to someone who can't afford to buy
the shirt.

01:12:53.840 --> 01:12:55.080
And if you decide later, you know what?

01:12:55.080 --> 01:12:57.160
Oh, I really liked that T-shirt.

01:12:57.160 --> 01:12:58.620
I really like that V-neck.

01:12:58.620 --> 01:13:00.270
I really liked that fill in the blank.

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:02.270
You can buy it again.

01:13:02.270 --> 01:13:07.620
And I rarely end up doing that, but every
once in a while I'll do a full purge where

01:13:07.620 --> 01:13:12.030
I'll just take garbage bags and garbage bags
of stuff and donate it to Goodwill or somewhere

01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:13.620
else.

01:13:13.620 --> 01:13:16.770
And then I'll realize, "Ah, okay, I kind of
got overzealous.

01:13:16.770 --> 01:13:19.610
I got rid of that one thing that I wore all
the time."

01:13:19.610 --> 01:13:21.590
And then I'll just buy it again on Amazon.

01:13:21.590 --> 01:13:22.620
It's there two days later.

01:13:22.620 --> 01:13:23.620
It's fine.

01:13:23.620 --> 01:13:27.130
Kevin Rose: Let me ask you, I have a very
personal question and something that I haven't

01:13:27.130 --> 01:13:31.400
shared before that I'd like to ask you about
buying things and-

01:13:31.400 --> 01:13:32.840
Tim Ferriss: Oh boy.

01:13:32.840 --> 01:13:33.840
Wow.

01:13:33.840 --> 01:13:38.050
Kevin Rose: No, I just think that this is
something that nobody talks about.

01:13:38.050 --> 01:13:44.661
And I always like to, you're so good at finding
stuff that you can pick up and you're a very

01:13:44.661 --> 01:13:49.949
open person, which I've always appreciated
about your podcast and just how you are transparent

01:13:49.949 --> 01:13:54.580
about feelings and emotions and a lot of things
that I think a lot of men could use to look

01:13:54.580 --> 01:13:57.600
up to in terms of-
Tim Ferriss: Oh God, is this question going

01:13:57.600 --> 01:13:58.750
to be a fucking hydrogen bomb?

01:13:58.750 --> 01:14:00.679
Kevin Rose: This is a tough one.

01:14:00.679 --> 01:14:01.800
No, no, no.

01:14:01.800 --> 01:14:06.130
So here's a problem that I've had, just being
completely honest.

01:14:06.130 --> 01:14:07.130
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:14:07.130 --> 01:14:12.790
Kevin Rose: There was, back when I was in
my 20s and early 30s, Digg was doing quite

01:14:12.790 --> 01:14:18.719
well, which was my startup for those of you
who don't know, it was early social news website

01:14:18.719 --> 01:14:20.490
that was, it was killing it.

01:14:20.490 --> 01:14:23.070
We had 38 million people a month that were
visiting on the site.

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:24.150
Tim Ferriss: Huge.

01:14:24.150 --> 01:14:26.530
Kevin Rose: Back then, it was one of the top
sites on the internet.

01:14:26.530 --> 01:14:28.300
It was crazy.

01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:33.720
It started to go sideways and it started to
go down and we were losing to Reddit.

01:14:33.720 --> 01:14:38.090
Reddit was starting to kick our ass in some
certain ways and we had dominated them for

01:14:38.090 --> 01:14:39.090
many years.

01:14:39.090 --> 01:14:42.200
And then all of a sudden that was not the
case and we were kind of reeling a little

01:14:42.200 --> 01:14:43.469
bit.

01:14:43.469 --> 01:14:51.460
I had sold a tiny bit of my stock, enough
to be able to afford an apartment and actually

01:14:51.460 --> 01:14:54.580
buy and put a down payment on an apartment
and that's how I got my Paige Street place

01:14:54.580 --> 01:14:55.580
in SF.

01:14:55.580 --> 01:15:01.520
And I remember thinking to myself, I need
to buy myself something to like, it was a

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:03.350
search to feel better.

01:15:03.350 --> 01:15:07.696
And so I went out and I actually bought, and
this is going to sound super douchey, but

01:15:07.696 --> 01:15:08.696
I'm okay to say this today.

01:15:08.696 --> 01:15:10.010
Actually I bought a Porsche.

01:15:10.010 --> 01:15:15.700
I bought a Porsche 911 back in the day.

01:15:15.700 --> 01:15:22.340
And looking back on that, I realized I was
just in a really difficult, sad state.

01:15:22.340 --> 01:15:27.770
It was hard for me to watch this baby of mine
and I didn't know how to course correct it.

01:15:27.770 --> 01:15:31.130
I was just so immature in a bunch of different
ways.

01:15:31.130 --> 01:15:35.040
But I was buying for the sake of trying to
fill a gap.

01:15:35.040 --> 01:15:40.010
Don't get me wrong, I still think vintage
Porsches and old Porsches and Porsches in

01:15:40.010 --> 01:15:41.239
general, they're just beautiful cars.

01:15:41.239 --> 01:15:43.300
I think they're great.

01:15:43.300 --> 01:15:49.239
I don't have a 911 today, but I will say that
I'd noticed the same thing.

01:15:49.239 --> 01:15:52.090
Tim Ferriss: Kevin is not anti-Porsche in
his group text messaging.

01:15:52.090 --> 01:15:53.429
Kevin Rose: I'm not anti-Porsche.

01:15:53.429 --> 01:15:54.770
Please don't cancel me, Porsche.

01:15:54.770 --> 01:15:56.820
I would love to be sponsored if you want to
sponsor me.

01:15:56.820 --> 01:15:59.700
I'm happy to drive a 911.

01:15:59.700 --> 01:16:03.010
So that said, here's the crazy thing.

01:16:03.010 --> 01:16:07.860
I went and ran Hokinkee, the luxury watch
brand company, I was CEO there for a few years

01:16:07.860 --> 01:16:15.070
and loved my experience there because we were
covering mechanical, like a dying art.

01:16:15.070 --> 01:16:17.380
It was mechanical timepieces.

01:16:17.380 --> 01:16:20.239
And it was very beautiful in its purest form.

01:16:20.239 --> 01:16:25.590
In its purest form, you talk to an artisan
that is a single independent watchmaker that

01:16:25.590 --> 01:16:30.940
takes a year and a half to build something
from scratch and sells 10 a year.

01:16:30.940 --> 01:16:31.940
That's beautiful.

01:16:31.940 --> 01:16:32.940
Those days are going away.

01:16:32.940 --> 01:16:37.590
In its douchey form, you're talking to Lambo
drivers that just want to have a flex that

01:16:37.590 --> 01:16:42.150
they can hold the watch on their wrist that
says I have $5 million on my wrist.

01:16:42.150 --> 01:16:43.150
That's a real thing.

01:16:43.150 --> 01:16:52.860
And so I met with a lot, as CEO of this company,
it still is the largest watch brand editorial

01:16:52.860 --> 01:16:54.739
site in the world.

01:16:54.739 --> 01:16:58.480
And you would meet all these collectors and
they would come up to you and they come in

01:16:58.480 --> 01:16:59.480
all different shapes and sizes.

01:16:59.480 --> 01:17:04.790
And it was clear to me that some of these
dudes, bless them, they were trying to fill

01:17:04.790 --> 01:17:09.050
that same thing that I was trying to fill,
where you go out and you spend money on something

01:17:09.050 --> 01:17:13.600
to make yourself feel in a group, to feel
connection.

01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:16.890
Because you'd come out and you'd say, "These
are other collectors like me."

01:17:16.890 --> 01:17:19.420
And it's tough.

01:17:19.420 --> 01:17:24.150
And I'm not anti-watch, I'm wearing an Apple
Watch today, but I still have a few time pieces

01:17:24.150 --> 01:17:29.239
that are really meaningful to me, including
one that my dad left me when he passed away.

01:17:29.239 --> 01:17:33.340
And I still love that whole thing.

01:17:33.340 --> 01:17:39.250
But how do you approach this as somebody,
have you ever spent your money on things?

01:17:39.250 --> 01:17:41.810
And then how do you approach it with...

01:17:41.810 --> 01:17:43.280
Have you ever done that?

01:17:43.280 --> 01:17:47.889
And then also, how do you approach that with
significant others?

01:17:47.889 --> 01:17:52.900
I've had this conversation with my wife about,
"Hey, we don't have to wear the flashy stuff.

01:17:52.900 --> 01:17:53.960
It's okay to wear..."

01:17:53.960 --> 01:18:00.219
I told her the other night, one of my favorite
times to hang out with you is when it's jeans

01:18:00.219 --> 01:18:02.719
and T-shirts version of you at a bar.

01:18:02.719 --> 01:18:03.719
I love that.

01:18:03.719 --> 01:18:04.719
Like having a beer.

01:18:04.719 --> 01:18:05.719
Tim Ferriss: Totally.

01:18:05.719 --> 01:18:07.469
Kevin Rose: We don't have to be fancy LA.

01:18:07.469 --> 01:18:08.989
I don't want that.

01:18:08.989 --> 01:18:11.810
Where do you stand on all this stuff?

01:18:11.810 --> 01:18:18.360
Tim Ferriss: So I think, and you've probably
observed this in me.

01:18:18.360 --> 01:18:19.431
Kevin Rose: You're so good at this.

01:18:19.431 --> 01:18:21.000
This is why I'm trying to pick your brain.

01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:22.620
You don't have to do any flashy shit.

01:18:22.620 --> 01:18:23.860
Tim Ferriss: Well, I'm good, I'm good.

01:18:23.860 --> 01:18:26.219
But there is a downside.

01:18:26.219 --> 01:18:32.850
There is a trade-off and I have not figured
out how to contend with this.

01:18:32.850 --> 01:18:39.909
And I'll actually mention also a book that
has come up several times in the last week

01:18:39.909 --> 01:18:47.780
from multiple people I respect and remember
Rick Rubin, the music producer, said to me,

01:18:47.780 --> 01:18:53.159
when that happens to him, he feels like it's
a signal from the universe that he needs to

01:18:53.159 --> 01:18:54.199
take a look at something.

01:18:54.199 --> 01:19:02.420
And so I kind of feel like that right now
with this book, which I'll come to in a second.

01:19:02.420 --> 01:19:12.850
I have had very few instances of buying expensive
things for myself.

01:19:12.850 --> 01:19:14.110
And you've seen this.

01:19:14.110 --> 01:19:15.469
Kevin Rose: I know.

01:19:15.469 --> 01:19:21.760
Tim Ferriss: I don't think I'm stingy in all
things.

01:19:21.760 --> 01:19:28.210
For instance, I will go to, if I think the
experience is going to leave an indelible

01:19:28.210 --> 01:19:34.190
mark in my mind, I will go to a place like
Alinea in Chicago and have a meal.

01:19:34.190 --> 01:19:36.280
And I did that and I'm really glad I did.

01:19:36.280 --> 01:19:38.210
And it's very expensive.

01:19:38.210 --> 01:19:39.930
I would consider it very expensive.

01:19:39.930 --> 01:19:44.100
It's like 1,500, 2,000 bucks for a meal.

01:19:44.100 --> 01:19:53.040
And that on some level would make the 15-year-old
version of me go into a seizure.

01:19:53.040 --> 01:20:00.010
I did not grow up with a lot of money and
grew up in a household where scarcity of money

01:20:00.010 --> 01:20:01.010
was an issue.

01:20:01.010 --> 01:20:02.440
Kevin Rose: Oh my God.

01:20:02.440 --> 01:20:06.550
My dad would yell at my mom for spending too
much money and stuff because we would literally

01:20:06.550 --> 01:20:07.550
bounce checks.

01:20:07.550 --> 01:20:10.850
I remember that being a conversation, like
the account is overdrawn.

01:20:10.850 --> 01:20:12.380
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

01:20:12.380 --> 01:20:13.929
It was a source of stress.

01:20:13.929 --> 01:20:15.630
It was a source of stress.

01:20:15.630 --> 01:20:24.450
And so I decided pretty early on as a kid,
okay, I do not want this type of stress in

01:20:24.450 --> 01:20:25.450
my life.

01:20:25.450 --> 01:20:27.920
That means I need to figure out how to make
money.

01:20:27.920 --> 01:20:33.320
And that in a way was the spark behind my
entrepreneurial experiments.

01:20:33.320 --> 01:20:35.440
Kevin Rose: That's a great point.

01:20:35.440 --> 01:20:43.710
Tim Ferriss: This type of stress that I have
been immersed in and exposed to, and I don't

01:20:43.710 --> 01:20:44.710
think it traumatized me.

01:20:44.710 --> 01:20:45.710
I wouldn't put it that way.

01:20:45.710 --> 01:20:52.739
I wouldn't want to remove the power of that
word trauma by applying it to something like

01:20:52.739 --> 01:20:53.739
this.

01:20:53.739 --> 01:20:56.889
And I think it kind of disgusted me how overused
the word trauma is.

01:20:56.889 --> 01:20:58.490
That's a separate thing.

01:20:58.490 --> 01:21:00.310
But it affected me.

01:21:00.310 --> 01:21:03.780
It definitely imprinted something on me.

01:21:03.780 --> 01:21:16.340
And as a result, I have spent relatively little
money celebrating anything.

01:21:16.340 --> 01:21:21.100
The fact that I spent, like these Japanese
saddles, I bought them at auction from, I

01:21:21.100 --> 01:21:29.510
can't remember, it was Christie's or Sotheby's,
and I think it was $5,000 a piece.

01:21:29.510 --> 01:21:43.130
And to allow myself to do that, the hurdle
was, not only do I have to finish and publish

01:21:43.130 --> 01:21:48.739
a book that is 500 plus pages, I think it
was maybe 700 pages, which was cut down from

01:21:48.739 --> 01:21:50.160
probably 1,500 pages.

01:21:50.160 --> 01:21:53.480
Kevin Rose: Was this in your head or something
you wrote down?

01:21:53.480 --> 01:21:57.489
Like this was a hurdle that you had to-
Tim Ferriss: It was in my head.

01:21:57.489 --> 01:22:04.050
But I said, if I publish this book and it
is number one New York Times, I will allow

01:22:04.050 --> 01:22:10.370
myself, as a reward, to buy these saddles
that cost $5,000 each.

01:22:10.370 --> 01:22:14.330
And if I don't, I'm not allowed to do this.

01:22:14.330 --> 01:22:24.220
So on some level I respect that and it's very
strict, high standards.

01:22:24.220 --> 01:22:26.530
And on some level all of those things are
good.

01:22:26.530 --> 01:22:37.610
But what I will say is when I meet or spend
time with friends of mine who have achieved

01:22:37.610 --> 01:22:42.970
some degree of success, and it's not always
millions of dollars.

01:22:42.970 --> 01:22:49.850
They've just figured out a job and a skill
where they've developed a career and they're

01:22:49.850 --> 01:22:58.030
stable and they fix a lot of problems for
themselves and they add a lot of joy for themselves

01:22:58.030 --> 01:23:01.730
and their families by spending money.

01:23:01.730 --> 01:23:06.929
The dark side of what I'm describing is that
I don't think I've developed that very well.

01:23:06.929 --> 01:23:11.710
And so I have more money than I know what
to do with.

01:23:11.710 --> 01:23:15.440
And I think I put it, I don't want to sound
like a prick.

01:23:15.440 --> 01:23:21.190
That doesn't mean that I'm just doing backstroke
through a pool full of gold coins like Scrooge

01:23:21.190 --> 01:23:22.190
McDuck.

01:23:22.190 --> 01:23:23.290
That's not what I'm saying.

01:23:23.290 --> 01:23:27.469
I think I am a very, very, hopefully this
doesn't sound pompous.

01:23:27.469 --> 01:23:33.010
I think I am a very, very good capital allocator
and steward of money.

01:23:33.010 --> 01:23:44.620
I think I use what I have to very high leverage
means and objectives through funding the science

01:23:44.620 --> 01:23:48.740
and the journalism fellowship with Michael
Pollan and so on.

01:23:48.740 --> 01:23:56.810
But here's what I'll say, Kevin, is there's
a dark side to it in that I don't use money

01:23:56.810 --> 01:23:58.679
to fix problems that I should fix.

01:23:58.679 --> 01:24:00.110
Kevin Rose: Like what?

01:24:00.110 --> 01:24:01.540
Give me an example.

01:24:01.540 --> 01:24:06.620
Tim Ferriss: I'll give you an example which
would be, there are little things...

01:24:06.620 --> 01:24:08.100
I'll give you a perfect example.

01:24:08.100 --> 01:24:16.960
So there is an article, it's not really an
article, it's more an adaptation of John Stuart

01:24:16.960 --> 01:24:20.550
Mill's ideas on free speech, which is an illustrated
edition.

01:24:20.550 --> 01:24:22.030
It's called All Minus One.

01:24:22.030 --> 01:24:25.530
You can find it on Amazon, you can also get
it for free.

01:24:25.530 --> 01:24:30.360
I think it's at heterodoxacademy.org/mill.

01:24:30.360 --> 01:24:37.350
And this came about through a conversation
with Jonathan Haidt, who's fascinating thinker

01:24:37.350 --> 01:24:41.639
and professor and researcher who wrote The
Coddling of the American Mind and many other

01:24:41.639 --> 01:24:43.119
books.

01:24:43.119 --> 01:24:51.699
So this is, let's see, on Kindle, it's I think
78 pages.

01:24:51.699 --> 01:25:01.690
And I agonized over when I was going to find
time to print this and put it together in

01:25:01.690 --> 01:25:04.080
a hard copy so that I could mark it up.

01:25:04.080 --> 01:25:06.580
Because I didn't want to wait for the paper
back.

01:25:06.580 --> 01:25:08.390
This is stupid.

01:25:08.390 --> 01:25:09.600
I have employees.

01:25:09.600 --> 01:25:13.420
I could send this to someone and just be like,
"Hey, figure this out.

01:25:13.420 --> 01:25:15.880
Whatever makes it readable, go."

01:25:15.880 --> 01:25:16.990
It would've taken 30 seconds.

01:25:16.990 --> 01:25:23.150
But there's part of me, for whatever reason,
that gets stuck on doing it myself and doesn't

01:25:23.150 --> 01:25:24.650
even consider that.

01:25:24.650 --> 01:25:27.540
It doesn't enter my head as an option.

01:25:27.540 --> 01:25:30.640
And there are many other examples of this.

01:25:30.640 --> 01:25:36.719
I've been to friends' houses where they love,
let's not name names, but well, because you'll

01:25:36.719 --> 01:25:37.719
probably get it.

01:25:37.719 --> 01:25:42.690
But I have a friend who really loves Topo
Chico and you go to his house and he always

01:25:42.690 --> 01:25:46.440
has the refrigerator drawer full of Topo Chico.

01:25:46.440 --> 01:25:47.440
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

01:25:47.440 --> 01:25:48.440
I love that.

01:25:48.440 --> 01:25:49.440
Tim Ferriss: It's never empty.

01:25:49.440 --> 01:25:50.440
He never runs out.

01:25:50.440 --> 01:25:51.440
Kevin Rose: It's extra sparkly.

01:25:51.440 --> 01:25:52.440
It's good.

01:25:52.440 --> 01:25:53.440
Tim Ferriss: It is.

01:25:53.440 --> 01:25:54.560
Yeah, it'll definitely take all the enamel
off your teeth.

01:25:54.560 --> 01:25:55.739
It's delicious.

01:25:55.739 --> 01:26:01.780
And even though all the hipster Austinites
have boycotted it, because it's owned by the

01:26:01.780 --> 01:26:08.450
evil empire of Coca-Cola now so they're drinking
Richard's Rainwater and others, which are

01:26:08.450 --> 01:26:09.450
fine.

01:26:09.450 --> 01:26:10.820
Fine, fine, fine sparkling water.

01:26:10.820 --> 01:26:14.540
But I just find the outrage pretty hilarious.

01:26:14.540 --> 01:26:18.530
But the point is, he has a team.

01:26:18.530 --> 01:26:23.060
He pays people to help him and his family
with their lives in various ways so that he

01:26:23.060 --> 01:26:29.219
can focus on things he's really good at, which
does not include figuring out at the last

01:26:29.219 --> 01:26:33.350
minute, "Oh shit, I'm out of Topo Chico, how
am I going to make it in time to the grocery

01:26:33.350 --> 01:26:38.020
store to buy this and chew up 45 minutes of
my time?"

01:26:38.020 --> 01:26:44.840
I don't have many of those systems in place,
and it's not because I am consciously deciding

01:26:44.840 --> 01:26:46.429
I need to save money.

01:26:46.429 --> 01:26:54.130
It's like the option doesn't even appear on
my mental UI to choose.

01:26:54.130 --> 01:26:58.889
So this is something I'm trying to figure
out, which leads to the book, which has been

01:26:58.889 --> 01:27:02.550
recommended a number of times now, which is
called Die With Zero.

01:27:02.550 --> 01:27:03.550
I've not read it.

01:27:03.550 --> 01:27:04.550
Kevin Rose: I've heard about this, actually.

01:27:04.550 --> 01:27:06.530
Tim Ferriss: So full disclosure now.

01:27:06.530 --> 01:27:08.130
Kevin Rose: Yeah, Chris Hutchens sent this
to me.

01:27:08.130 --> 01:27:09.389
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no shit.

01:27:09.389 --> 01:27:10.389
Okay.

01:27:10.389 --> 01:27:18.179
So Bill Perkins is the author, and I should
know better than to recommend something before

01:27:18.179 --> 01:27:19.179
I've read it.

01:27:19.179 --> 01:27:22.699
So I just want to make it super clear, I have
not read this.

01:27:22.699 --> 01:27:30.320
But the book is Die With Zero: Getting All
You Can From Your Money and Your Life.

01:27:30.320 --> 01:27:39.469
And it's written by Bill Perkins, who's a
famous energy trader/investor and The Wall

01:27:39.469 --> 01:27:40.800
Street Journal bestseller.

01:27:40.800 --> 01:27:46.170
I'll just read a little bit of the description,
which is very seductive to me because it's

01:27:46.170 --> 01:27:49.520
written by someone who has also been an operator.

01:27:49.520 --> 01:27:54.659
If this book were written by just someone
who's waxing poetic without any real bonafides,

01:27:54.659 --> 01:27:57.160
bonafides, I would be skeptical.

01:27:57.160 --> 01:28:02.800
But this is someone who's actually been a
real operator in the trenches.

01:28:02.800 --> 01:28:07.110
So the description is a common sense guide
to living rich instead of dying rich.

01:28:07.110 --> 01:28:09.940
Imagine if by the time you died you did everything
you were told to.

01:28:09.940 --> 01:28:13.710
You worked hard, saved your money, and looked
forward to financial freedom when you retired.

01:28:13.710 --> 01:28:16.429
The only thing you wasted along the way was...

01:28:16.429 --> 01:28:17.429
Your life.

01:28:17.429 --> 01:28:21.659
Die With Zero presents a startling new and
provocative philosophy as well as practical

01:28:21.659 --> 01:28:24.110
guide on how to get the most out of your money
and the most out of your life.

01:28:24.110 --> 01:28:27.380
It's intended for those who place lifelong
memorable experiences far ahead of simply

01:28:27.380 --> 01:28:30.540
making and accumulating money for ones so-called
golden years.

01:28:30.540 --> 01:28:31.540
Kevin Rose: Yes.

01:28:31.540 --> 01:28:32.540
Tim Ferriss: And it goes on.

01:28:32.540 --> 01:28:39.070
This is interesting to me and I'm hoping that
it provides me with some counter programming

01:28:39.070 --> 01:28:41.920
that will maybe help me to do more.

01:28:41.920 --> 01:28:52.590
And I have, I will say, been finding some
outlets where I don't have hangups, and one

01:28:52.590 --> 01:28:58.030
is spending money on art or supporting artists.

01:28:58.030 --> 01:29:05.659
So I will be doing, moving forward quite a
few experiments with artists and artwork.

01:29:05.659 --> 01:29:09.159
I did one that is actually running right now
as we record.

01:29:09.159 --> 01:29:10.880
It's going to end in a few hours.

01:29:10.880 --> 01:29:15.969
But it's a competition for AI generated or
enhanced artwork.

01:29:15.969 --> 01:29:18.090
Kevin Rose: Cockpunch-related or no?

01:29:18.090 --> 01:29:19.489
Tim Ferriss: Cockpunch related.

01:29:19.489 --> 01:29:20.719
Kevin Rose: Are you serious?

01:29:20.719 --> 01:29:21.719
Tim Ferriss: So they have to work...

01:29:21.719 --> 01:29:22.719
Yeah, yeah.

01:29:22.719 --> 01:29:23.719
CockPunch related.

01:29:23.719 --> 01:29:28.760
And the stuff that has come out is beyond
belief.

01:29:28.760 --> 01:29:35.170
It is hard to wrap my head around and I say
this as someone who has quite a bit of art

01:29:35.170 --> 01:29:40.050
background and worked as an illustrator, paid
a lot of bills as an illustrator for magazines

01:29:40.050 --> 01:29:42.360
and books in college.

01:29:42.360 --> 01:29:48.110
It is hard to wrap my head around what this
technology will do for creative expression.

01:29:48.110 --> 01:29:49.110
And it's controversial.

01:29:49.110 --> 01:29:50.110
Kevin Rose: Oh, big time.

01:29:50.110 --> 01:29:58.440
Tim Ferriss: I have seen blowback because
AI, whether it's ChatGPT for text or other

01:29:58.440 --> 01:30:00.950
tools requires training data.

01:30:00.950 --> 01:30:02.500
So where do you get that training data?

01:30:02.500 --> 01:30:10.850
If it's graphical expression, if it's what
we would consider visual art, they might be

01:30:10.850 --> 01:30:13.260
pulling from, say, artists on Art Station.

01:30:13.260 --> 01:30:14.260
Kevin Rose: Right.

01:30:14.260 --> 01:30:18.480
But it's blending it so well together that
you could never pick out individually which

01:30:18.480 --> 01:30:19.969
artist is pulling from.

01:30:19.969 --> 01:30:26.560
Tim Ferriss: Sometimes, unless in your prompt
you say, instead of in the style of Van Gogh,

01:30:26.560 --> 01:30:28.570
which most people would consider fair game.

01:30:28.570 --> 01:30:32.440
If it's in the style of fill in the blank
contemporary artists who makes their living

01:30:32.440 --> 01:30:39.199
selling prints in part or doing commissioned
artwork, is that net positive or a net negative

01:30:39.199 --> 01:30:41.390
for them?

01:30:41.390 --> 01:30:42.390
I don't know.

01:30:42.390 --> 01:30:45.159
Kevin Rose: You now who you should talk to,
you know who's fun to chat with this is, I

01:30:45.159 --> 01:30:49.350
was talking to Mike Shinoda about this, our
mutual friend from Lincoln Park, and he was

01:30:49.350 --> 01:30:55.869
talking about how there's this list that you
can sign up for that apparently will exclude

01:30:55.869 --> 01:30:57.949
you from the training data if you really want
out of it.

01:30:57.949 --> 01:30:59.420
Tim Ferriss: It's like the do not call list.

01:30:59.420 --> 01:31:00.719
Kevin Rose: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

01:31:00.719 --> 01:31:05.220
He was saying that because in music you could
imagine this is going to be a big deal.

01:31:05.220 --> 01:31:08.960
You're going to say, hey-
Tim Ferriss: I'm already seeing Stable Diffusion

01:31:08.960 --> 01:31:13.040
applied to music in ways that make your head
spin.

01:31:13.040 --> 01:31:14.219
And it's so early.

01:31:14.219 --> 01:31:15.369
It's not even the first inning.

01:31:15.369 --> 01:31:16.369
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

01:31:16.369 --> 01:31:19.739
I was asking him about like, "Hey, what do
you think of AI when it says, Hey, play me

01:31:19.739 --> 01:31:23.100
a song like it was written by Mike Shinoda."

01:31:23.100 --> 01:31:25.170
And what does that sound like?

01:31:25.170 --> 01:31:27.880
And it's a big topic to go into.

01:31:27.880 --> 01:31:28.880
Tim Ferriss: How does he feel?

01:31:28.880 --> 01:31:29.880
How does he feel?

01:31:29.880 --> 01:31:39.310
Kevin Rose: I don't want to speak for him,
but at that time, I got the sense, and I'm

01:31:39.310 --> 01:31:42.170
just paraphrasing here, I'm actually not even
going to paraphrase.

01:31:42.170 --> 01:31:47.590
I'm going to say that I got the sense that
it's early days and if anything I know about

01:31:47.590 --> 01:31:51.610
Mike is definitely he embraces the future.

01:31:51.610 --> 01:31:52.930
There's no doubt about it.

01:31:52.930 --> 01:31:58.530
He's all Web3, he's doing NFT drops, he's
doing [inaudible 01:33:30] stuff with music.

01:31:58.530 --> 01:32:00.949
He's awesome on that front.

01:32:00.949 --> 01:32:06.219
But at the same time, I can imagine if you're
an artist in his position, which is just the

01:32:06.219 --> 01:32:12.619
top tier, top 1% of all musical artists out
there, you also want to protect who you are

01:32:12.619 --> 01:32:13.810
as an individual in your IP.

01:32:13.810 --> 01:32:19.591
Now the sense I got from him is figuring out
how this is going to weave and what you're

01:32:19.591 --> 01:32:21.850
going to have to do in the future.

01:32:21.850 --> 01:32:24.350
So, he'd be a fun one to have on and eventually
talk about this stuff.

01:32:24.350 --> 01:32:25.900
Tim Ferriss: I should have him back on.

01:32:25.900 --> 01:32:27.850
I had him on the podcast like 100 years ago.

01:32:27.850 --> 01:32:29.560
Kevin Rose: Oh, he's such a good dude.

01:32:29.560 --> 01:32:32.010
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, great guy.

01:32:32.010 --> 01:32:36.850
On the AI front, I will say, I have artists
in my family.

01:32:36.850 --> 01:32:38.860
I see how hard it is.

01:32:38.860 --> 01:32:43.030
I've been there myself.

01:32:43.030 --> 01:32:50.930
I should point out also that I am not necessarily
immune to the influence of these technologies,

01:32:50.930 --> 01:32:59.659
in the sense that I have seen blog posts generated
using predominantly AI with very few prompts

01:32:59.659 --> 01:33:01.400
and things are going to change for writers.

01:33:01.400 --> 01:33:02.600
Kevin Rose: Oh my God, it's so scary.

01:33:02.600 --> 01:33:04.550
Tim Ferriss: In a big way.

01:33:04.550 --> 01:33:08.780
That will particularly apply to non-fiction
writers, and I am principally a non-fiction

01:33:08.780 --> 01:33:09.780
writer.

01:33:09.780 --> 01:33:13.350
Kevin Rose: The fiction writers, dude, a lot
of these tools are being done around fiction.

01:33:13.350 --> 01:33:14.449
Tim Ferriss: Fiction.

01:33:14.449 --> 01:33:15.530
Fiction is going to be harder.

01:33:15.530 --> 01:33:22.800
I think fiction will be a lot harder to thread,
but both will be affected.

01:33:22.800 --> 01:33:29.110
So there are many questions that this prompts,
pun intended.

01:33:29.110 --> 01:33:37.429
One is, what are the factors that will drive,
say reading, in my case?

01:33:37.429 --> 01:33:43.260
How much of it will be being certain that
you are reading something generated by a human

01:33:43.260 --> 01:33:48.469
before you're willing to commit to having
an emotional response, even if the output

01:33:48.469 --> 01:33:50.180
is identical?

01:33:50.180 --> 01:33:55.340
Will there be some authenticity of human production
that becomes important?

01:33:55.340 --> 01:33:56.610
I could see that becoming important.

01:33:56.610 --> 01:33:57.610
Kevin Rose: Interesting.

01:33:57.610 --> 01:34:02.580
Tim Ferriss: Do you really want to cry watching
a movie that was 100% produced by robots?

01:34:02.580 --> 01:34:03.580
Maybe not.

01:34:03.580 --> 01:34:08.520
Maybe that is a hard line that people draw
where they're like, I don't actually want

01:34:08.520 --> 01:34:10.790
to have my emotions manipulated by machines.

01:34:10.790 --> 01:34:15.320
Kevin Rose: Yeah, but you already do today
with the graphics, right?

01:34:15.320 --> 01:34:16.320
Those are created by machines.

01:34:16.320 --> 01:34:17.320
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:34:17.320 --> 01:34:18.320
Oh yeah.

01:34:18.320 --> 01:34:19.960
So no, it's a question of degree.

01:34:19.960 --> 01:34:21.920
Kevin Rose: Let me ask you question-
Tim Ferriss: We're already being affected.

01:34:21.920 --> 01:34:27.560
Kevin Rose: What you're saying, Tim, is, let's
just jump forward 20 years from now, you're

01:34:27.560 --> 01:34:33.199
saying there is a world where you could imagine
a novel that would have some type of designation

01:34:33.199 --> 01:34:39.260
on it digitally that would say this was human
written, just to ensure-

01:34:39.260 --> 01:34:40.920
Tim Ferriss: Oh, absolutely.

01:34:40.920 --> 01:34:42.590
Kevin Rose: That's so crazy.

01:34:42.590 --> 01:34:43.590
That's awesome.

01:34:43.590 --> 01:34:44.680
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely.

01:34:44.680 --> 01:34:54.100
Imagine you have these time pieces that are
one of a kind, produced by the labor of one

01:34:54.100 --> 01:34:58.449
or a handful of people over a year and a half.

01:34:58.449 --> 01:35:05.480
The origin and the story matter.

01:35:05.480 --> 01:35:12.900
You could produce something maybe even superior
with advanced robotics and software and so

01:35:12.900 --> 01:35:13.900
on.

01:35:13.900 --> 01:35:19.280
But you want the physical and psychic imprint.

01:35:19.280 --> 01:35:21.989
Kevin Rose: It's the human connection.

01:35:21.989 --> 01:35:22.989
Yeah, exactly.

01:35:22.989 --> 01:35:23.989
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:23.989 --> 01:35:26.280
So I think absolutely.

01:35:26.280 --> 01:35:33.560
Just you have a stamp for organic or humane
certified or free trade, it'd be like human-generated

01:35:33.560 --> 01:35:34.560
stamp.

01:35:34.560 --> 01:35:38.520
I would be surprised if that's not a thing.

01:35:38.520 --> 01:35:42.690
Kevin Rose: You should go in and file your
trademarks and patents.

01:35:42.690 --> 01:35:47.340
Tim Ferriss: So, that'll be a thing.

01:35:47.340 --> 01:35:48.610
Let me add a few more though.

01:35:48.610 --> 01:35:58.050
So another one will be, actually this is more
philosophical, so I thought about the counter-arguments

01:35:58.050 --> 01:36:03.780
and the blowback related to AI before I launched
this competition.

01:36:03.780 --> 01:36:10.520
I understood the counter-arguments, and I
don't disagree with them.

01:36:10.520 --> 01:36:14.200
This is going to affect the competitive landscape
for artists, period.

01:36:14.200 --> 01:36:15.200
Full stop.

01:36:15.200 --> 01:36:16.230
Kevin Rose: Sure.

01:36:16.230 --> 01:36:19.610
Tim Ferriss: Especially for things like logo
design, things of this type.

01:36:19.610 --> 01:36:20.610
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

01:36:20.610 --> 01:36:21.640
Of course, Fiverr is fucked.

01:36:21.640 --> 01:36:22.640
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:36:22.640 --> 01:36:26.780
Like website layout and so on.

01:36:26.780 --> 01:36:39.260
The way I'm looking at it is if I, Tim, wanted
to help as many artists as possible, what

01:36:39.260 --> 01:36:41.360
would my chess move be?

01:36:41.360 --> 01:36:51.340
Where I landed was we are going from the horse
and the horse drawn carriage to the car.

01:36:51.340 --> 01:36:56.510
That transition is happening whether we want
it to or not.

01:36:56.510 --> 01:37:00.449
If we own a bunch of horses, if we own a bunch
of carriages, if we own the equivalent of

01:37:00.449 --> 01:37:04.260
a taxi service back in the day, that's fine.

01:37:04.260 --> 01:37:06.159
But the technology is going to change.

01:37:06.159 --> 01:37:14.940
So if you want to be in a competitive position,
if you want to have advantages, you need to

01:37:14.940 --> 01:37:19.900
be on the front end of learning about these
technologies.

01:37:19.900 --> 01:37:25.730
Which is part of the reason why I wanted to
do this competition, to say, hey guys, you

01:37:25.730 --> 01:37:31.790
can make this work for you in a lot of ways,
but you got to be on the early end.

01:37:31.790 --> 01:37:32.790
You just have to.

01:37:32.790 --> 01:37:37.170
Kevin Rose: There's a couple things that I
think are important to point out here in that,

01:37:37.170 --> 01:37:45.190
to me, this just represents another leap forward
in technology.

